From a493164e10b80ea07b5bb5013ff67aef86b35210 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 20:47:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Add an in-portal PDF reader to the Library

Library items gain a "Read" button beside "Download", opening the document
in a pdf.js reader with page navigation, zoom, fit-to-width, selectable
text and full-text search.

pdf.js is not cheap, so it is behind React.lazy the way the 3D atlas is:
the main bundle grows by ~3 kB, and the 373 kB reader chunk plus its
worker load only when someone actually opens a document. The browser's
built-in PDF frame would have been a fraction of the code but renders only
the first page on iOS, which rules it out for textbook reading on an iPad.

Pages render as they scroll into view and are released again once well off
screen, so a 900-page text does not try to rasterise 900 canvases. Device
pixel ratio is capped at 2.

Search marks the matched characters rather than the whole text run pdf.js
happened to emit, and counts occurrences rather than pages, so "3 of 19"
means what a reader expects. Counting walks the same text runs the
highlighter does, so the counter and the marks on the page cannot drift
apart. A phrase split across two runs is neither marked nor counted --
those boundaries are pdf.js's, not ours.

Viewing signs a 4-hour URL rather than the 1-hour download link: pdf.js
keeps fetching byte ranges as the reader scrolls, and an hour-old link
would strand someone mid-book.

Verified in headless Chromium at desktop and iPhone viewports: page
raster, lazy render and release, zoom steps, page and match navigation,
substring-only marks, and clean teardown of marks when the query is
cleared.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01HV2TSLxfnBFbXmi2V2hHHf
